window.BOOK_DATA=window.BOOK_DATA||{};BOOK_DATA["ch01.en"]={"id":"ch01","lang":"en","title":"Function","titleTa":"செயற்கூறு","pages":[{"page":1,"steps":[{"say":"Vanakkam! I am Guru, your robot tutor. Welcome to Unit 1, Chapter 1, called Function. In Tamil, we say seyarkooru. I am happy to learn this with you today.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Unit 1 - Chapter 1: Function","body":""}]},{"say":"By the end of this chapter, you will know what a function is, what parameters and arguments are, the difference between interface and implementation, pure functions, and side effects. Today, on page one, we start with the introduction and the idea of a subroutine.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Function, parameters, arguments","body":""},{"kind":"point","title":"Interface vs implementation, pure functions, side effects","body":""}]},{"say":"The book says that the time taken to finish a task is the most important factor when we write and evaluate an algorithm. This time should not depend on the programming language, the compiler, or the computer we use. Algorithms are carried out using the statements of a programming language.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Algorithm time should not depend on language, compiler or computer","body":""}]},{"say":"Sometimes, more than one statement must be repeated many times to do one task. For this, we use subroutines. A subroutine is a small block of a program used to do one particular task again and again.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Subroutine","body":"A small block of code used to do a particular task again and again."}]},{"say":"Subroutines are the basic building blocks of computer languages. In programming languages, these subroutines are called functions. This is very important for your exam.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Function","body":"In programming languages, subroutines are called functions."}]},{"say":"Now section 1.2. A function is a unit of code that is defined within a bigger program. It contains blocks of code that work on different kinds of inputs, such as variables and expressions, and give a consistent output.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Function = unit of code inside a larger program","body":""},{"kind":"point","title":"Inputs: variables, expressions. Output: consistent","body":""}]},{"say":"Next, function specification. Look at a colon equal to open bracket 24 close bracket. Here, the 24 is not an expression. It is a definition. A definition binds a value to a name. So here, the value 24 is bound to the name a. Definitions are not expressions, and expressions are not definitions. They are different syntactic blocks. But a definition can contain expressions inside it, and the reverse can also happen.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Definition binds value to name","body":"a:=(24)"},{"kind":"warning","title":"A definition is not an expression","body":""}]},{"say":"Finally, a key pair of terms. Parameters are the variables in a function definition. Arguments are the values passed to the function definition. We will see examples on the next page. Remember this pair for the board exam.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Parameter","body":"A variable in the function definition."},{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Argument","body":"A value passed to the function definition."}]}],"explainAgain":[{"say":"Think of a school canteen. Every day, the same steps repeat: take the order, collect the money, give the snack. Instead of explaining all steps each time, the canteen has one fixed routine. That routine is like a function.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Function = a reusable routine, like a canteen routine","body":""}]},{"say":"Now parameters and arguments. A parameter is like an empty slot on the order form, named snack. An argument is what you actually write there, say samosa. The slot is the parameter. The actual value is the argument.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Parameter = empty slot; argument = actual value filled in","body":""}]},{"say":"And a definition is like writing a name on a tiffin box. You are tying a value to a name. In a colon equal to 24, the name a is tied to the value 24.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Definition = tying a value to a name","body":""}]}],"example":[{"say":"Let us see a worked example. Suppose a program must find the square of numbers many times. Instead of writing x times x again and again, we write it once as a function and call it with different values. Here x is the parameter. When we call square with 5, the value 5 is the argument, and the answer is 25. Calling with 6 gives 36.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Square function","body":"let square x :=\n  return: x * x\n\nsquare 5  -> 25\nsquare 6  -> 36"},{"kind":"example","title":"x is the parameter; 5 and 6 are arguments","body":""}]}],"checkQuestion":{"question":"What is a subroutine, and what is it called in programming languages? Also, what is the difference between a parameter and an argument?","idealAnswer":"A subroutine is a small block of a program used to do a particular task again and again. In programming languages, subroutines are called functions. Parameters are the variables in a function definition. Arguments are the values passed to the function definition.","keywords":["subroutine","function","parameter","argument","துணை நிரல்","செயற்கூறு"]}},{"page":2,"steps":[{"say":"On the last page, we learned that a function is a small reusable block of code. Now let us see how a function is written, and what parameters and arguments are.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Parameters and Arguments","body":""}]},{"say":"A parameter is a variable in the function definition. An argument is the value that we pass to the function when we call it. This is a very common board exam question.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Parameter","body":"Variable in the function definition"},{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Argument","body":"Value passed to the function"}]},{"say":"Look at the power function in the book. It has a requires line, which says b must be zero or more. It has a returns line, which says the answer is a to the power of b. Then the definition uses let rec, name pow, and parameters a and b. If b is zero it gives 1. Otherwise it multiplies a with another call.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Function without data types","body":"(requires: b>=0 )\n(returns: a to the power of b)\nlet rec pow a b:=\n    if b=0 then 1\n    else a * pow b (a-1)"}]},{"say":"Notice that we did not write any data type here. Some compilers can find the types by themselves. For other compilers, writing the type is compulsory. In this example, the if expression gives 1, so its type is int, and so the function returns int. Since b is compared with zero, b is int. Since a is multiplied, a is also int.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"No data type given: compiler infers a, b and result as int","body":""}]},{"say":"Now the same function with data types. This is called type annotation. When we write type annotations, the brackets are a must. Writing types clearly helps us to understand error messages.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Function with data types","body":"(requires: b> = 0 )\n(returns: a to the power of b )\nlet rec pow (a: int) (b: int) : int :=\n    if b=0 then 1\n    else a * pow b (a-1)"},{"kind":"warning","title":"Type annotation needs round brackets ( )","body":""}]},{"say":"Now the syntax. The word let introduces the definition. Then comes the function name, then the parameters, then the assignment symbol, and then the expression. For a function that calls itself, we use let rec instead of let. Such a function is called a recursive function. If it is not recursive, we can leave out rec.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Syntax of function definition","body":"let rec fn a1 a2 ... an := k"},{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Recursive function","body":"A function that calls itself"}]},{"say":"Here is the factorial example. If n is zero, it returns 1. Otherwise it multiplies n with factorial of n minus 1. Also remember, in a definition like a assigned 24, the value 24 is bound to the name a. A definition is not an expression.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Factorial","body":"(Requires: n>= 0)\nlet rec fact n :=\n    if n = 0 then 1\n    else\n        n * fact(n-1)"}]},{"say":"Finally, function types. x arrow y means one input of type x and output of type y. x1 arrow x2 arrow y means two inputs and one output. Also remember, all functions are static. There is no dynamic function definition. Well done, you are doing great!","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"x → y : one input type x, output type y","body":""},{"kind":"point","title":"x1 → x2 → y : two inputs, output y","body":""}]}],"explainAgain":[{"say":"Think of a school canteen counter. The counter has a rule: you give a token and it gives you a snack. The empty space for the token in the rule is the parameter. Your actual token, say number 5, is the argument.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Parameter = empty slot in the rule","body":""},{"kind":"point","title":"Argument = actual value you give","body":""}]},{"say":"Now think of a recursive function like a student asking a friend in the next bench to solve a smaller sum. That friend asks the next one, and so on, until someone gets a very easy one, like zero. That easy case is the stopping point. Without it, the chain never ends.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Recursive = calls itself on a smaller problem, stops at base case","body":""}]}],"example":[{"say":"Let us trace factorial of 3. Factorial of 3 is 3 times factorial of 2. Factorial of 2 is 2 times factorial of 1. Factorial of 1 is 1 times factorial of 0. Factorial of 0 is 1, which stops the calls. Now multiply back: 1, then 1, then 2, then 6. Here n is the parameter and 3 is the argument.","board":[{"kind":"example","title":"fact 3 = 3 * fact 2 = 3 * 2 * fact 1 = 3 * 2 * 1 * fact 0 = 6","body":"fact 0 = 1, so the answer is 6"}]}],"checkQuestion":{"question":"What is the difference between a parameter and an argument? Also, which keyword do we use to define a recursive function?","idealAnswer":"A parameter is a variable in the function definition. An argument is the value passed to the function when it is called. A recursive function, which calls itself, is defined using let rec.","keywords":["parameter","argument","variable","value passed","let rec"]}},{"page":3,"steps":[{"say":"On the last page we saw how a function is defined, with parameters and arguments, and the let rec syntax. Now we continue with recursive functions, function types, and then the idea of interface versus implementation.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Recursive Functions, Function Types, Interface","body":""}]},{"say":"A function that calls itself is called a recursive function. This is why we write let rec, where rec stands for recursive. This is an important definition for your board exam.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Recursive function","body":"A function definition that calls itself"}]},{"say":"Here is the factorial example. It requires that n is greater than or equal to zero. If n is zero, the answer is one. Otherwise, the answer is n multiplied by factorial of n minus one. The case n equals zero stops the recursion.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Factorial","body":"(Requires: n>= 0)\nlet rec fact n :=\n    if n = 0 then 1\n    else\n        n * fact(n-1)"}]},{"say":"Next, function types. The type x arrow y means the function takes one input of type x and returns an output of type y. The type x1 arrow x2 arrow y takes two inputs, of types x1 and x2, and returns type y. In general, x1 to xn arrow y means n inputs and one output of type y.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Function type syntax","body":"x → y\nx1 → x2 → y\nx1 → ... → xn → y"}]},{"say":"Remember this note. All functions are static definitions. There is no such thing as a dynamic function definition.","board":[{"kind":"warning","title":"All function definitions are static, none are dynamic","body":""}]},{"say":"Now, interface versus implementation. An interface is the set of actions an object can perform. Think of a light switch. You press it and the bulb glows. You do not need to know how it glows. In object oriented programming, an interface is the description of all the functions an object supports. A bulb-like object would have turn_on and turn_off.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Interface","body":"Set of actions an object can perform; defines them but does not do them"},{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Implementation","body":"Carries out the instructions defined in the interface"}]},{"say":"Please note the difference. The interface defines what an object can do, but does not actually do it. The implementation carries out those instructions. This difference often comes in the two mark questions.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Interface = what can be done; Implementation = how it is done","body":""}]}],"explainAgain":[{"say":"Think of a school bell routine. A teacher says, find the factorial of five. To do this, you need factorial of four, and for that factorial of three, and so on, until you reach zero, which is simply one. Then you multiply back up. That is a function calling itself on a smaller problem.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Recursion: solve a smaller copy of the same problem until a stopping case","body":""}]},{"say":"For interface, think of a TV remote. The buttons are the interface. The circuits inside the TV are the implementation. You only press buttons and never worry about the circuits.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Remote buttons = interface; inside circuits = implementation","body":""}]}],"example":[{"say":"Let us trace fact of three. Fact three is three times fact two. Fact two is two times fact one. Fact one is one times fact zero. Fact zero is one, so the recursion stops. Multiplying back, one times one is one, two times one is two, and three times two is six. So fact of three is six.","board":[{"kind":"example","title":"fact 3 = 3 * fact 2 = 3 * 2 * fact 1 = 3 * 2 * 1 * fact 0 = 3 * 2 * 1 * 1 = 6","body":""}]}],"checkQuestion":{"question":"What is a recursive function, and which keyword is used to define it in the textbook syntax?","idealAnswer":"A recursive function is a function that calls itself. It is defined using the keyword let rec, where rec stands for recursive.","keywords":["calls itself","let rec","rec","recursive","தற்சுழற்சி"]}},{"page":4,"steps":[{"say":"On the last page we saw what an interface is, using the example of a light switch. Now we continue with the properties of an interface and the car example.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"1.3.1 Properties of Interface","body":""}]},{"say":"First, the class template specifies the interface that is needed to create an object properly and to make it work. In object oriented languages, the class is where the interface is described.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Class template specifies the interface needed to create and use an object","body":""}]},{"say":"Second, by passing function calls to an object, we can control its properties and attributes. We only call the function. We do not touch the insides.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Calling functions on an object controls its behaviour","body":""}]},{"say":"Now the car example. A driver wants to increase the speed. He does not need to know how the engine works inside. He just presses the accelerator.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Accelerator","body":"The interface between the driver (calling object) and the engine (called object)."}]},{"say":"The calling function would be speed of 70. This is the interface. Inside, the engine mixes fuel, air, pressure and electricity to create power and move the vehicle. The driver is shielded from all this. So the interface is separated from the implementation.","board":[{"kind":"diagram","title":"Car example","body":"flowchart TD\nA[\"Driver calls speed(70)\"] --> B{\"Required speed reached?\"}\nB -->|\"No\"| C[\"Engine pulls more fuel\"]\nC --> B\nB -->|\"Yes\"| D[\"Return\"]"}]},{"say":"The page ends with a simple implementation. The function min takes three values x, y and z, and finds the smallest using nested if conditions. First it compares x and y. Then the smaller one is compared with z.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"min of three values","body":"let min x y z :=\n if x < y then\n  if x < z then x else z\n else\n  if y < z then y else z"}]},{"say":"For the board exam, remember this. The interface defines what can be done. The implementation actually does it. This difference is asked often, so write it with the car or bulb example.","board":[{"kind":"warning","title":"Interface only defines actions. Implementation performs them.","body":""}]}],"explainAgain":[{"say":"Think of a TV remote. You press the volume button and the sound increases. You do not know the circuits inside the TV.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Remote buttons = interface","body":""}]},{"say":"The buttons are the interface. The circuits inside which really change the volume are the implementation. The company can change the circuits, but you still press the same button.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Circuits inside = implementation","body":""}]},{"say":"In the same way, the accelerator is the interface of a car, and the engine is the implementation.","board":[]}],"example":[{"say":"Let us trace the min function with x equal to 5, y equal to 3, z equal to 4. First, is x less than y? 5 is not less than 3, so we go to the else part. There we check whether y is less than z. 3 is less than 4, so the answer is y, which is 3.","board":[{"kind":"example","title":"min 5 3 4","body":"x<y? 5<3 No\ny<z? 3<4 Yes\nResult = 3"}]}],"checkQuestion":{"question":"In the car example, what is the interface and what is the implementation?","idealAnswer":"The accelerator is the interface between the driver and the engine. The engine, which uses fuel, air and electricity to move the car, is the implementation. The interface is separated from the implementation.","keywords":["accelerator","interface","engine","implementation"]}},{"page":5,"steps":[{"say":"On the last page we saw interface versus implementation, and we met pure functions that give the same answer for the same arguments. Now we see why pure functions are so useful.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Pure functions: benefits and impure functions","body":""}]},{"say":"The first benefit is speed. If a function is pure, calling it again with the same arguments gives the same answer. So the compiler needs to call it only once and can reuse the result.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Pure function: same arguments, same result, so call only once","body":""}]},{"say":"The book shows a function called length. It sets i to zero, and then checks whether i is less than strlen of s. Each time the check runs, strlen of s is called, and strlen has to go through the whole string s.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Length function from the book","body":"let length s:=\n    i: = 0\n    if i <strlen (s) then\n    -- Do something which doesn't affect s\n    ++i"}]},{"say":"If strlen is pure, and s is not changed inside the loop, the compiler can remove the extra calls. The loop then calls strlen only one time.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"strlen pure and s unchanged: unnecessary calls removed","body":""}]},{"say":"Why is strlen pure? It takes one argument, the string s, and calculates its length. It reads from memory but does not change any values. The value it returns depends only on that input.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"strlen is pure","body":"Takes one argument, reads memory, changes nothing, result depends only on the argument"}]},{"say":"Remember this note for the exam. When a pure function is evaluated, there is no side effect.","board":[{"kind":"warning","title":"Pure function: no side effects","body":""}]},{"say":"Next, impure functions. A function that depends on variables or functions outside its own definition may not behave the same on every call. Even without arguments, something inside it can cause side effects. Such a function is called an impure function.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Impure function","body":"Depends on outside things, so the same call may give different results, and it may cause side effects"}]},{"say":"The book's example is random. Each call to random can give a different value. So in the book's code, a is random, and the function returns a if it is above ten, else it returns ten. We cannot say for sure what comes out. Learn this example for your exam.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Impure example: random","body":"let a:= random()\n    if a > 10 then\n        return: a\n    else\n        return: 10"}]}],"explainAgain":[{"say":"Think of a pure function like a school calculator. Press seven times seven and you get forty nine, every single time. The calculator needs nothing else to decide the answer.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Pure = calculator: same input, same output","body":""}]},{"say":"Because the answer never changes, the compiler can remember it. It need not recompute it again and again. That is like writing down a result in your notebook and just looking at it later.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Same answer, so compute once and reuse","body":""}]},{"say":"An impure function is like rolling a dice. The same action can give a different result each time. Random and date are such functions.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Impure = dice: result can change","body":""}]}],"example":[{"say":"Let us take the strlen example step by step. Suppose s is the word school. A loop keeps checking whether i is less than strlen of s. If strlen is not known to be pure, it counts the letters every time, so six letters are counted again and again. Since strlen is pure and s never changes, the answer six stays the same. So it is calculated once and reused.","board":[{"kind":"example","title":"s = school: strlen gives 6 every time, so compute once","body":""}]}],"checkQuestion":{"question":"Why is strlen called a pure function?","idealAnswer":"Strlen takes only one argument, the string, and calculates its length. It reads from memory but does not change any values, so the same argument always gives the same result, with no side effects.","keywords":["same","argument","length","no side effect","does not change"]}},{"page":6,"steps":[{"say":"On the last page we met pure functions and impure functions, and we saw that side effects are not always bad. Now let us see a clear example of a side effect.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Side Effects, Pure vs Impure, GCD","body":""}]},{"say":"Here is the textbook example. A variable y is set to zero outside the function. The function inc takes an integer x, adds x to y, and returns y. So the function changes a variable that lives outside it.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Side effect example","body":"y := 0\nlet inc (x: int) : int :=\n  y := y + x\n  return (y)"}]},{"say":"Because y changes inside the function, the answer is different every time we call it. Calling inc with 1 gives 1 the first time, then 2, then 3. The side effect of inc is changing the outside variable y.","board":[{"kind":"warning","title":"Same input, different output means impure","body":""}]},{"say":"Some side effects are easy to spot, and some need careful thinking. Now the table of differences. A pure function depends only on its arguments. Same arguments always give the same result, and it has no side effects. It does not change its arguments.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Pure function","body":"Output depends only on arguments. Same input gives same output. No side effects."}]},{"say":"An impure function does not depend only on its arguments. The same arguments can give different results. Examples are random and date. It may also change its arguments. This difference is very important for the board exam.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Impure function","body":"Output may differ for same input. Examples: random(), date(). May change arguments."}]},{"say":"Next, the gcd function, a pure function that finds the greatest common divisor of two positive integers. If b is not zero, it calls itself with b and the remainder of a divided by b. Otherwise it returns a. This is a recursive function.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"GCD function","body":"let rec gcd a b :=\n if b <> 0 then gcd b (a mod b)\n else\n return a"}]},{"say":"The outputs are as follows. gcd of 13 and 27 gives 1. gcd of 20536 and 7826 gives 2. The function keeps calling itself until b becomes zero. Remember that it passes b and a mod b as the new a and b.","board":[{"kind":"example","title":"gcd 13 27 gives 1; gcd 20536 7826 gives 2","body":""}]}],"explainAgain":[{"say":"Think of a pure function like a calculator's square button. Press it with 5 and you always get 25. Nothing else changes.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Pure = calculator square button","body":""}]},{"say":"An impure function is like a school canteen token counter. Each time you ask, the number you get is different, because a counter outside keeps changing. That is the variable y in inc. Changing something outside the function is a side effect.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Impure = counter that changes each time","body":""}]},{"say":"And gcd is like a repeated division game. You keep replacing the pair with b and the remainder, until the remainder is zero. The last non-zero number is your answer.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"gcd: repeat until b is 0, then answer is a","body":""}]}],"example":[{"say":"Let us trace gcd 20 and 8. Here b is 8, not zero, so we call gcd of 8 and 20 mod 8, which is 4. Now b is 4, so we call gcd of 4 and 8 mod 4, which is 0. Now b is zero, so the function returns a, which is 4. So gcd of 20 and 8 is 4.","board":[{"kind":"example","title":"Trace of gcd 20 8","body":"gcd 20 8 -> gcd 8 4 -> gcd 4 0 -> returns 4"}]}],"checkQuestion":{"question":"In the example with the function inc and the outside variable y, why is inc an impure function, and what is its side effect?","idealAnswer":"Inc is impure because it changes the outside variable y, so the same input gives different results on different calls. Its side effect is changing the value of the external variable y.","keywords":["impure","side effect","outside variable","changes y","different output"]}},{"page":7,"steps":[{"say":"On the last page we saw pure and impure functions, and the gcd example. Now we use a function to solve the Chameleons of Chromeland problem you studied in Class 11.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Chameleons of Chromeland using a function","body":""}]},{"say":"Let a, b and c be the number of chameleons of three colours. Their starting values are capital A, B and C. The input condition is that a equals b. When two chameleons of different colours meet, both change to the third colour. At the end, all chameleons must show one colour.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Input condition","body":"a = b, with a = A, b = B, c = C"}]},{"say":"The algorithm is named monochromatize, and we write it as monochromatize of a, b, c. The output relation is that a and b both become zero, and c becomes A plus B plus C.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Output: a = b = 0 and c = A + B + C","body":""}]},{"say":"In every iteration, one chameleon of each of the two equal groups meets and changes to the third colour. So a goes down by one, b goes down by one, and c goes up by two. Look at the table with 4, 4 and 6. After four iterations we get 0, 0 and 14. Note that 4 plus 4 plus 6 is also 14.","board":[{"kind":"example","title":"A, B, C = 4, 4, 6","body":"iteration 0: 4 4 6\niteration 1: 3 3 8\niteration 2: 2 2 10\niteration 3: 1 1 12\niteration 4: 0 0 14"}]},{"say":"The flowchart takes input a, b, c. It checks whether a is greater than zero. If true, it updates a, b, c to a minus 1, b minus 1, c plus 2, and repeats. If false, a and b are zero and c is A plus B plus C, and we output a, b, c.","board":[{"kind":"diagram","title":"Monochromatize flowchart","body":"flowchart TD\nA[\"Input a, b, c\"] --> B{\"a > 0 ?\"}\nB -- \"True\" --> C[\"a, b, c := a-1, b-1, c+2\"]\nC --> B\nB -- \"False\" --> D[\"Output a, b, c\"]"}]},{"say":"Now the function as written in the book. It is a recursive function, so it uses let rec. If a is greater than zero, we update a, b and c. Otherwise we call monochromatize again. Finally it returns a, b, c. Please copy the code exactly as in your textbook for the exam.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"monochromatize function","body":"let rec monochromatize a b c :=\n    if a > 0 then\n        a, b, c := a-1, b-1, c+2\n    else\n        monochromatize a b c\n    return a, b, c"}]}],"explainAgain":[{"say":"Think of a class with three groups of students: red, green and blue. Red and green have equal numbers. Each time one red and one green student meet, both change to blue house. So red and green reduce by one, and blue grows by two.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Two equal groups join the third group","body":""}]},{"say":"We repeat this until red and green are zero. Then everyone is in blue. The total number of students never changes. That is why c finally equals A plus B plus C.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Total A + B + C stays the same","body":""}]}],"example":[{"say":"Worked example with A, B, C equal to 2, 2 and 3. Start: 2, 2, 3. After one meeting: 1, 1, 5. After two meetings: 0, 0, 7. The output is a equals b equals 0 and c equals 7, which is 2 plus 2 plus 3.","board":[{"kind":"example","title":"A, B, C = 2, 2, 3","body":"0: 2 2 3\n1: 1 1 5\n2: 0 0 7\nc = 2+2+3 = 7"}]}],"checkQuestion":{"question":"In each iteration of the monochromatize algorithm, how do the values of a, b and c change, and what are the final values?","idealAnswer":"In each iteration a decreases by one, b decreases by one and c increases by two. At the end a and b are zero and c equals A plus B plus C.","keywords":["a decreases by 1","b decreases by 1","c increases by 2","a = b = 0","A+B+C"]}},{"page":8,"steps":[{"say":"In the last page we saw the monochromatize function that solves the chameleons problem using a function. Now this final page is the chapter summary. Let us revise every key idea, because these are very important for the board exam.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Chapter Summary: Function","body":""}]},{"say":"First, algorithms are carried out using the statements of a programming language. When some statements must be repeated many times, we put them in a subroutine. A subroutine is a small part of a program used again and again for one particular task. In programming languages, subroutines are called functions.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Subroutine","body":"Small part of a program used repeatedly for a particular task"},{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Function","body":"A unit of code, usually defined within a bigger program"}]},{"say":"A function works on many kinds of inputs, like variables and expressions, and gives a consistent output. Also remember that definitions have distinct syntax blocks. A definition binds a value to a name. It is not an expression.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Function: many inputs, consistent output","body":""},{"kind":"point","title":"Definition binds a value to a name; it is not an expression","body":""}]},{"say":"Next, parameters and arguments. Parameters are the variables in the function definition. Arguments are the values passed to the function definition. When we write type annotations in a function definition, the brackets are compulsory. This is a favourite one mark question.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Parameters","body":"Variables in the function definition"},{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Arguments","body":"Values passed to the function definition"},{"kind":"warning","title":"Type annotations need parentheses ( )","body":""}]},{"say":"Now interface and implementation. An interface is the set of actions an object can do. It defines the actions, but does not actually perform them. Implementation carries out the instructions defined in the interface. Think of the accelerator of a car. The pedal is the interface. The engine working inside is the implementation.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Interface","body":"Set of actions an object can do; only defines them"},{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Implementation","body":"Carries out the instructions defined in the interface"}]},{"say":"Finally, pure and impure functions. A pure function gives the same answer whenever the same arguments are passed, and it has no side effects. An example is sin of zero, or the square function. A function that has side effects, even without parameters, is an impure function. Examples are random and date. Changing a variable outside the function is a side effect.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Pure function","body":"Same arguments, same result; no side effects"},{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Impure function","body":"Has side effects; result may differ, e.g. random(), date()"}]},{"say":"Well done! If you remember these points, you can answer the two mark, three mark and five mark questions of this chapter confidently.","board":[]}],"explainAgain":[{"say":"Think of a function like a tuition teacher's fixed method for solving one type of sum. You give the numbers, and the method gives you the answer. You can use the same method again and again, so you do not rewrite the steps each time.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Function = reusable method for one task","body":""}]},{"say":"Parameters are the empty boxes written in the question paper. Arguments are the actual numbers you fill in. An interface is like the buttons on a calculator. Implementation is the circuit inside that does the work.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Parameter = empty box; Argument = value filled in","body":""}]},{"say":"A pure function is like a vending machine with a fixed price. Same coin, same biscuit, every time. An impure function is like a lucky draw. The same ticket may give a different result each time.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Pure = predictable; Impure = unpredictable or changes outside things","body":""}]}],"example":[{"say":"Let us decide whether two functions are pure or impure. The first function, square, returns x times x. The second function, inc, adds x to an outside variable y and returns y. Square gives the same output for the same input, so it is pure. Inc changes the outside variable y, so the answer changes every call. So inc is impure, and its side effect is changing y.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Pure: square","body":"let square x:=\n return: x * x"},{"kind":"code","title":"Impure: inc","body":"y: = 0\nlet inc (x: int): int: =\n y: = y + x\nreturn (y)"}]}],"checkQuestion":{"question":"What is the difference between a pure function and an impure function?","idealAnswer":"A pure function always returns the same result for the same arguments and has no side effects, for example square or sin of zero. An impure function may give different results for the same arguments or change outside variables, so it has side effects, for example random or date.","keywords":["same result","same arguments","side effects","impure","pure"]}}],"concepts":["Subroutines and Functions","Function Definition and Syntax","Parameters and Arguments","Recursive Functions","Interface vs Implementation","Pure Functions","Impure Functions and Side Effects"],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","concept":"Subroutines and Functions","question":"What is a small part of a program that is used repeatedly to perform a particular task called?","code":"","options":["Subroutine","Files","Pseudo code","Modules"],"answer":"Subroutine","keywords":[],"explanation":"A subroutine is a small block of code used again and again for a specific task. In programming languages, subroutines are called functions."},{"id":"q2","type":"short","concept":"Subroutines and Functions","question":"What is a function in a programming language?","code":"","options":[],"answer":"A function is a unit of code defined within a larger program. It contains blocks of code that work on inputs like variables and expressions to give a consistent output.","keywords":["unit of code","larger program","inputs","output"],"explanation":"The textbook defines a function as a unit of code within a larger program that works on various inputs to give a consistent output."},{"id":"q3","type":"mcq","concept":"Function Definition and Syntax","question":"In a = (24), what does the definition do?","code":"","options":["It binds the value 24 to the name a","It compares a with 24","It makes 24 an expression","It creates a recursive function"],"answer":"It binds the value 24 to the name a","keywords":[],"explanation":"Definitions bind values to names. Here 24 is bound to the name a. Definitions are not expressions."},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","concept":"Function Definition and Syntax","question":"Which special word introduces a function definition in the syntax of this chapter?","code":"","options":["let","def","define","func"],"answer":"let","keywords":[],"explanation":"A definition is introduced with the keyword let, followed by the function name, its parameters and the expression after :=."},{"id":"q5","type":"short","concept":"Function Definition and Syntax","question":"Why are brackets compulsory when writing type annotations for parameters?","code":"","options":[],"answer":"When we write data types for the parameters, the brackets ( ) are required in the function definition, as in (a: int) (b: int).","keywords":["type annotation","brackets","parentheses","data type"],"explanation":"The textbook says parentheses are necessary when writing type annotations of parameters."},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","concept":"Parameters and Arguments","question":"What are the values passed to a function definition called?","code":"","options":["Arguments","Parameters","Subroutines","Modules"],"answer":"Arguments","keywords":[],"explanation":"Parameters are the variables in the function definition. Arguments are the values passed to it."},{"id":"q7","type":"short","concept":"Parameters and Arguments","question":"Differentiate between parameters and arguments.","code":"","options":[],"answer":"Parameters are the variables in a function definition. Arguments are the values passed to the function when it is called.","keywords":["variables","function definition","values passed"],"explanation":"This is a basic board question. Parameters belong to the definition, arguments belong to the call."},{"id":"q8","type":"mcq","concept":"Recursive Functions","question":"Which keyword is used to define a recursive function?","code":"","options":["let rec","let","rec only","loop"],"answer":"let rec","keywords":[],"explanation":"A function that calls itself is recursive. It is defined using let rec instead of let."},{"id":"q9","type":"short","concept":"Recursive Functions","question":"In the gcd function, which statement stops the recursion?","code":"let rec gcd a b :=\n if b <> 0 then gcd b (a mod b)\n else\n return a","options":[],"answer":"The else part, return a, ends the recursion when b becomes 0.","keywords":["return a","b = 0","else"],"explanation":"The function keeps calling itself until b is 0. Then it returns a."},{"id":"q10","type":"mcq","concept":"Interface vs Implementation","question":"Which of these defines what an object can do, but does not actually do it?","code":"","options":["Interface","Implementation","Compiler","Operating system"],"answer":"Interface","keywords":[],"explanation":"An interface defines the actions an object can perform. Implementation carries out the commands defined in the interface."},{"id":"q11","type":"short","concept":"Interface vs Implementation","question":"Differentiate interface and implementation with an example.","code":"","options":[],"answer":"An interface only defines what an object can do, like the accelerator of a car. Implementation actually carries out those actions, like the engine doing the work.","keywords":["defines actions","carries out","accelerator","engine"],"explanation":"The driver uses the accelerator, which is the interface, without knowing the engine's inner working."},{"id":"q12","type":"mcq","concept":"Pure Functions","question":"A function that always gives the same result for the same arguments and has no side effects is called:","code":"","options":["Pure function","Impure function","Partial function","Dynamic function"],"answer":"Pure function","keywords":[],"explanation":"Examples are sin(0), which is always 0, and square x."},{"id":"q13","type":"short","concept":"Pure Functions","question":"Why is strlen called a pure function?","code":"","options":[],"answer":"strlen takes one variable and finds its length. It only reads the input and does not change any values, so the same input always gives the same result.","keywords":["length","does not change","same input","same output"],"explanation":"Since strlen is pure, repeated calls can be avoided in a loop."},{"id":"q14","type":"short","concept":"Impure Functions and Side Effects","question":"What is a side effect? Explain with an example.","code":"y := 0\nlet inc (x: int): int :=\n y := y + x\n return (y)","options":[],"answer":"A side effect is a change made outside the function. In inc, the outside variable y is changed each time, so the result differs on every call.","keywords":["outside variable","changes y","different result","impure"],"explanation":"The inc function changes the external variable y, so it is impure."},{"id":"q15","type":"predict_output","concept":"Recursive Functions","question":"What is the output of gcd 13 27 for the textbook function?","code":"let rec gcd a b :=\n if b <> 0 then gcd b (a mod b)\n else\n return a","options":[],"answer":"1","keywords":[],"explanation":"13 and 27 have no common factor other than 1, so gcd is 1."},{"id":"q16","type":"predict_output","concept":"Pure Functions","question":"What is the output of gcd 20536 7826 as shown in the textbook?","code":"let rec gcd a b :=\n if b <> 0 then gcd b (a mod b)\n else\n return a","options":[],"answer":"2","keywords":[],"explanation":"The textbook output for these two numbers is 2."}],"reteach":{"Subroutines and Functions":[{"steps":[{"say":"Let us look at subroutines and functions in a fresh way. Think of a school bell system. The peon does not invent how to ring the bell every time. There is one fixed routine, and he repeats it whenever needed. A subroutine is just like that. It is a small block of code that does one particular job and can be used again and again.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Subroutines and Functions","body":""},{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Subroutine","body":"A small block of code used again and again to do a particular task"}]},{"say":"In programming languages, subroutines are called functions. So function and subroutine mean almost the same thing in this chapter. The textbook says a function is a unit of code defined within a bigger program. It can take inputs like variables or expressions, and it gives a fixed output.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Function","body":"A unit of code defined inside a larger program; takes inputs and gives a fixed output"}]},{"say":"Now a worked example. Suppose you must find the square of a number in ten places of your program. Instead of writing the multiplication ten times, you write one function called square. It takes x and returns x times x. Then you simply call it whenever you need it.","board":[{"kind":"example","title":"Square function","body":"let square x := return: x * x"}]},{"say":"Notice two words. The variable x in the function definition is called a parameter. The actual value you send, like 5, is called an argument. So square called with 5 gives 25. Parameters are in the definition. Arguments are the values passed in.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Parameter = variable in definition; Argument = value passed","body":""}]},{"say":"For the board exam, remember this. Subroutines are the basic building blocks of computer languages. They are used when more than one statement must be repeated many times. Also remember that a definition is not an expression. It binds a value to a name, like a colon equals twenty four binding the value twenty four to the name a.","board":[{"kind":"warning","title":"A definition is not an expression; it binds a value to a name","body":""}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Subroutines and Functions","question":"Why do we use a subroutine in a program?","code":"","options":["To repeat a particular task without rewriting the statements","To make the computer run slower","To remove all variables from the program","To store files on the disk"],"answer":"To repeat a particular task without rewriting the statements","keywords":[],"explanation":"A subroutine is a small block of code used repeatedly to do a specific task."},{"id":"p2","type":"short","concept":"Subroutines and Functions","question":"What are subroutines called in programming languages?","code":"","options":[],"answer":"In programming languages, subroutines are called functions.","keywords":["functions","subroutines"],"explanation":"The textbook says subroutines are known as functions in programming languages."}]},{"steps":[{"say":"Let us make this very simple, step by step. Imagine a tea stall. The tea maker has one recipe. Every time a customer asks for tea, he follows the same recipe. He does not make a new recipe each time. This recipe is like a function.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Function = a recipe you reuse","body":""}]},{"say":"Step one. Sometimes the same few lines of code must run many times. Writing them again and again is tiring and risky. Step two. So we put those lines in one small block and give it a name. This block is called a subroutine. In programming languages, we call it a function.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Same lines needed many times: make a named block","body":""},{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Subroutine = Function","body":"Small reusable block of code"}]},{"say":"Step three. A function can take inputs. In our tea stall, the inputs are milk and sugar. In code, the names for inputs inside the definition are parameters. The real values we give while calling are arguments. Step four. The function then gives back an output.","board":[{"kind":"diagram","title":"Input to output","body":"flowchart TD\nA[\"Arguments\"] --> B[\"Function\"]\nB --> C[\"Output\"]"}]},{"say":"Now common mistakes. First mistake: mixing up parameter and argument. Parameter is the variable in the definition. Argument is the value passed. Second mistake: thinking a function runs only once. It can be used many times. Third mistake: thinking a definition is an expression. It is not. It only binds a value to a name.","board":[{"kind":"warning","title":"Do not mix parameter with argument","body":""},{"kind":"warning","title":"A definition is not an expression","body":""}]},{"say":"You are doing well. Keep this in mind. A function is a unit of code defined in a bigger program, and it gives a fixed output for the inputs. This is a favourite two mark question.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Function: unit of code in a larger program","body":""}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Subroutines and Functions","question":"In a function definition, the variables written in the definition are called what?","code":"","options":["Parameters","Arguments","Interfaces","Side effects"],"answer":"Parameters","keywords":[],"explanation":"Parameters are the variables in the function definition. Arguments are the values passed."},{"id":"p2","type":"short","concept":"Subroutines and Functions","question":"What does the definition a := (24) tell us?","code":"","options":[],"answer":"It binds the value 24 to the name a. It is a definition, not an expression.","keywords":["binds","value","name","definition"],"explanation":"Definitions bind values to names, and they are not expressions."}]},{"steps":[{"say":"Let us start from the very basics. You already know that a program is a list of statements. Sometimes we need the same few statements again and again. What can we do? We can put them together in one small block.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Starting simple","body":""},{"kind":"point","title":"Program = list of statements","body":""}]},{"say":"That small block is called a subroutine. We give it a name. Whenever we need that work, we just use the name. In programming languages, a subroutine is called a function.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Function","body":"A named small block of code that does one task"}]},{"say":"Here is a very simple example. A function called square takes a number x. It gives back x times x. If we give 4, we get 16. If we give 5, we get 25. We wrote the code once, but used it many times.","board":[{"kind":"example","title":"Square","body":"let square x := return: x * x"}]},{"say":"Remember two words. The variable x in the definition is a parameter. The value like 4 that we give is an argument. If you remember this, you can answer many board questions. Well done for trying again!","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Parameter in definition, argument in call","body":""}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Subroutines and Functions","question":"In the function square x, which one is the argument when we call square with 4?","code":"let square x := return: x * x","options":["4","x","square","return"],"answer":"4","keywords":[],"explanation":"The value passed during the call, 4, is the argument. The variable x is the parameter."},{"id":"p2","type":"short","concept":"Subroutines and Functions","question":"Define a subroutine in one sentence.","code":"","options":[],"answer":"A subroutine is a small block of code used again and again to perform a particular task.","keywords":["small","block","code","repeat","task"],"explanation":"The textbook defines subroutines as small program blocks used repeatedly for a specific task."}]}],"Impure Functions and Side Effects":[{"steps":[{"say":"Let us look at impure functions again with a fresh idea. Think of a vending machine. A pure function is like a calculator. Same buttons, same answer, every time.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Impure Functions and Side Effects","body":""},{"kind":"point","title":"Pure: same input gives same output, no side effects","body":""}]},{"say":"An impure function is like a canteen counter where the price depends on the rush, or the stock left. The answer depends on something outside your input. It can also change things outside, like a shared register in the school office.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Impure function","body":"Output may differ for the same arguments, or it changes things outside the function."}]},{"say":"A side effect is any change the function makes to the outside world, for example changing a variable that lives outside the function. The book says a side effect is not always bad. Sometimes it is useful.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Side effect","body":"A change visible outside the function, such as changing an outside variable."},{"kind":"warning","title":"Side effects are not always harmful","body":""}]},{"say":"Now the worked example from the book. The variable y starts at zero. The function inc takes x, adds x to y, and returns y. Call inc with 5 and you get 5. Call inc with 5 again and you get 10. Same input, different output.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"inc function","body":"y := 0\nlet inc (x: int): int :=\n  y := y + x\n  return (y)"},{"kind":"example","title":"inc(5) gives 5, then inc(5) gives 10","body":""}]},{"say":"The side effect of inc is that it changes the outside variable y. Another book example is random, which gives different outputs for the same call. Remember this for the board exam: pure functions have no side effects, impure functions may have them.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Examples of impure: random(), date(), inc()","body":""}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Impure Functions and Side Effects","question":"Which of these is an impure function?","code":"","options":["random()","square(x) which returns x times x","sin(0)","a function returning the larger of two given numbers"],"answer":"random()","keywords":[],"explanation":"random() gives different outputs for the same call, so it is impure."},{"id":"p2","type":"predict_output","concept":"Impure Functions and Side Effects","question":"y starts at 0. What does the second call return?","code":"y := 0\nlet inc (x: int): int :=\n  y := y + x\n  return (y)\ninc(5)\ninc(5)","options":[],"answer":"10","keywords":[],"explanation":"The first call makes y 5. The second adds 5 more, so y and the result become 10."}]},{"steps":[{"say":"Let us go step by step in simple words. First, a function takes input and gives output. That is its job.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Function: input in, output out","body":""}]},{"say":"Think of a water tap in your school. If you turn it the same way, you expect the same flow. A pure function is like that. It depends only on what you give it.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Pure: depends only on its arguments","body":""}]},{"say":"Now think of a tap whose flow depends on what other classes are using at that time. You turn it the same way, but get different water. That is an impure function. It depends on things outside.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Impure: depends on outside things","body":""},{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Impure function","body":"Same arguments may give different results."}]},{"say":"A side effect means the function changes something outside itself. Like a student who borrows the class duster and moves it to another room. Others now see a change. In code, it is changing an outside variable.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Side effect","body":"Changing something outside the function."}]},{"say":"Common mistakes. First, do not think side effects are always bad. The book says they can be useful. Second, do not think a function is pure just because it has no arguments. Third, do not forget that random and date are impure.","board":[{"kind":"warning","title":"Side effects are not always bad","body":""},{"kind":"warning","title":"No arguments does not mean pure","body":""}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"short","concept":"Impure Functions and Side Effects","question":"What is a side effect of a function?","code":"","options":[],"answer":"A side effect is a change the function makes outside itself, such as changing an outside variable.","keywords":["change","outside","variable"],"explanation":"A side effect is an observable change outside the function, like modifying an outside variable."},{"id":"p2","type":"mcq","concept":"Impure Functions and Side Effects","question":"Which statement about side effects is correct according to the textbook?","code":"","options":["They are sometimes useful","They are always harmful","They happen only in pure functions","They never change outside variables"],"answer":"They are sometimes useful","keywords":[],"explanation":"The textbook says a side effect is not a harmful act and can be useful at times."}]},{"steps":[{"say":"Let us start from what you already know. A function is a small block of code that you can call again and again. Example: square of a number.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"square","body":"let square x :=\n  return: x * x"}]},{"say":"Square of 4 is always 16. Every time. This is a pure function. Its answer depends only on the input.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"square(4) is always 16: pure","body":""}]},{"say":"Now imagine a function that gives you the time right now. Ask twice and you get different answers. Same call, different result. This is an impure function.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"date() changes every time: impure","body":""}]},{"say":"Also, if a function changes a variable outside itself, that change is called a side effect. Remember two things: impure means different answers or outside changes. A side effect means a change outside the function.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Side effect","body":"A change made outside the function."},{"kind":"point","title":"Impure functions can have side effects","body":""}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Impure Functions and Side Effects","question":"A function gives different outputs for the same input. What type of function is it?","code":"","options":["Impure function","Pure function","Static function","Recursive function only"],"answer":"Impure function","keywords":[],"explanation":"Different outputs for the same arguments mean the function is impure."},{"id":"p2","type":"short","concept":"Impure Functions and Side Effects","question":"In the function inc, which outside variable is changed, and what is this called?","code":"y := 0\nlet inc (x: int): int :=\n  y := y + x\n  return (y)","options":[],"answer":"The outside variable y is changed, and this is called a side effect.","keywords":["y","side effect","outside"],"explanation":"inc changes the outside variable y, which is its side effect."}]}],"Recursive Functions":[{"steps":[{"say":"Let us look again at recursive functions. A recursive function is a function that calls itself. The textbook says: a function definition that calls itself is called a recursive function.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Recursive Function","body":""},{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Recursive function","body":"A function that calls itself"}]},{"say":"Here is a new analogy. Imagine you are standing in a long queue in school and want to know your position. You ask the student in front, how many are ahead of you? That student asks the next one, and so on. The first student says, I am number one. Then the answers come back down the line, each adding one.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Queue analogy: ask the person ahead, until the first one answers","body":""}]},{"say":"In a function, the first student is the stopping condition. It is also called the base case. Without it, the function would call itself forever. In the textbook, the keyword let rec is used to define a recursive function. The word rec is short for recursive.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"let rec","body":"Keyword used to define a recursive function"},{"kind":"warning","title":"No stopping condition means endless calls","body":""}]},{"say":"Now the worked example from the book: factorial. If n is zero, the answer is 1. Otherwise the answer is n times factorial of n minus 1.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Factorial","body":"(Requires: n>= 0)\nlet rec fact n :=\n    if n = 0 then 1\n    else\n        n * fact(n-1)"}]},{"say":"Let us trace fact of 3. It becomes 3 times fact of 2. That is 3 times 2 times fact of 1. That is 3 times 2 times 1 times fact of 0. Fact of 0 is 1. So the answer is 6. Remember this for the board exam.","board":[{"kind":"example","title":"fact 3 = 3 * 2 * 1 * 1 = 6","body":""}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Recursive Functions","question":"What is a recursive function?","code":"","options":["A function that calls itself","A function that has no parameters","A function that changes outside variables","A function that never returns a value"],"answer":"A function that calls itself","keywords":[],"explanation":"The textbook defines a recursive function as one that calls itself."},{"id":"p2","type":"predict_output","concept":"Recursive Functions","question":"What is the value of fact 4?","code":"let rec fact n :=\n    if n = 0 then 1\n    else\n        n * fact(n-1)","options":[],"answer":"24","keywords":[],"explanation":"4 * 3 * 2 * 1 * 1 = 24."}]},{"steps":[{"say":"Let us go slowly. Step one: a function is a small block of code that does one job. Step two: a recursive function is a function that uses itself inside its own body.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Recursive = function calls itself","body":""}]},{"say":"A different analogy: a stack of Russian dolls. You open one doll and find a smaller doll inside. You keep opening until you reach the smallest doll that cannot be opened. That smallest doll is the stopping point.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Dolls inside dolls: open until the smallest one","body":""}]},{"say":"Every recursive function has two parts. Part one is the stopping condition, which gives the answer directly. Part two is the self call, with a smaller input. In the book, the stopping part is if n equals 0 then 1. The self call is n times fact of n minus 1.","board":[{"kind":"diagram","title":"Two parts","body":"flowchart TD\nA[\"Call fact n\"] --> B{\"n = 0?\"}\nB -- \"Yes\" --> C[\"Return 1\"]\nB -- \"No\" --> D[\"n * fact(n-1)\"]\nD --> A"}]},{"say":"Now a simple example from the book's practice section: sum of numbers up to n. The sum of n is n plus the sum of n minus 1. When n is zero, the answer is zero.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Sum up to n","body":"let rec sum n :=\n    if n = 0 then 0\n    else n + sum(n-1)"}]},{"say":"Common mistakes. First, forgetting the stopping condition. Second, writing let instead of let rec. Third, not making the input smaller, like calling the same value again. The book's pow example had this error: it called pow b of a minus 1, instead of pow a of b minus 1.","board":[{"kind":"warning","title":"Mistakes: no base case, missing rec, input not getting smaller","body":""}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Recursive Functions","question":"Which keyword is used in the textbook to define a recursive function?","code":"","options":["let rec","let only","return","requires"],"answer":"let rec","keywords":[],"explanation":"The word rec after let tells that the function calls itself."},{"id":"p2","type":"short","concept":"Recursive Functions","question":"Why does a recursive function need a stopping condition?","code":"","options":[],"answer":"Without a stopping condition the function would keep calling itself forever; the stopping condition ends the recursion.","keywords":["stopping condition","calls itself","ends","forever"],"explanation":"The stopping condition gives a direct answer and ends the chain of calls."}]},{"steps":[{"say":"Let us start from what you already know. You know a function, like square of x, which returns x times x. Now think of a function that uses itself to do part of its work. That is a recursive function.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Recursive function calls itself","body":""}]},{"say":"Think of climbing stairs. To reach step 3, you first reach step 2. To reach step 2, you first reach step 1. Step 0 is the ground, where you start. Ground is where we stop.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Step 3 needs step 2, needs step 1, needs ground","body":""}]},{"say":"Here is the simplest recursive function. It finds the sum up to n. If n is zero, answer is zero. Otherwise, answer is n plus the sum up to n minus 1.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Simple example","body":"let rec sum n :=\n    if n = 0 then 0\n    else n + sum(n-1)"}]},{"say":"For sum of 2: it is 2 plus sum of 1. Sum of 1 is 1 plus sum of 0. Sum of 0 is 0. So the answer is 3.","board":[{"kind":"example","title":"sum 2 = 2 + 1 + 0 = 3","body":""}]},{"say":"Remember: use let rec, keep a stopping condition, and make the input smaller each time. Well done, you can do this!","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"let rec","body":"Defines a function that calls itself"}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"predict_output","concept":"Recursive Functions","question":"What is sum 3?","code":"let rec sum n :=\n    if n = 0 then 0\n    else n + sum(n-1)","options":[],"answer":"6","keywords":[],"explanation":"3 + 2 + 1 + 0 = 6."},{"id":"p2","type":"mcq","concept":"Recursive Functions","question":"In fact n, which statement stops the recursion?","code":"let rec fact n :=\n    if n = 0 then 1\n    else n * fact(n-1)","options":["if n = 0 then 1","n * fact(n-1)","let rec fact n","else"],"answer":"if n = 0 then 1","keywords":[],"explanation":"When n is 0, it returns 1 directly without calling itself."}]}],"Function Definition and Syntax":[{"steps":[{"say":"Let us look at function definitions again, with a fresh idea. Think of a function as a recipe card in your school canteen. The card has a name, a list of ingredients, and the steps to cook. Anyone can use the card again and again.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Function = a recipe card","body":""}]},{"say":"In our textbook, a function is written with the special word let. After let comes the function name. Then come the parameters, which are like the ingredient slots. Then the sign colon equals. After that comes the formula that gives the answer.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"let","body":"Special word that starts a function definition"},{"kind":"code","title":"General syntax","body":"let rec fn a1 a2 ... an := k"}]},{"say":"Here fn is the function name. a1 to an are the parameters. The letter k is the body, the formula that is calculated. The word rec is needed only when the function calls itself. Such a function is called a recursive function. If it does not call itself, we can leave out rec.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"rec only for recursive functions","body":""}]},{"say":"Now a worked example. The factorial function. It requires that n is zero or more. If n is zero, it gives 1. Otherwise it gives n multiplied by factorial of n minus 1. Since fact calls itself, we write let rec.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Factorial","body":"(Requires: n>= 0)\nlet rec fact n :=\n    if n = 0 then 1\n    else\n        n * fact(n-1)"}]},{"say":"Remember the difference. Parameters are the variables in the definition. Arguments are the actual values passed when we call it. This is very important for your board exam.","board":[{"kind":"warning","title":"Parameter = variable in definition; Argument = value passed","body":""}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Function Definition and Syntax","question":"Which special word is added to let when a function calls itself?","code":"","options":["rec","def","loop","self"],"answer":"rec","keywords":[],"explanation":"A recursive function is defined using let rec instead of let."},{"id":"p2","type":"short","concept":"Function Definition and Syntax","question":"In the syntax let rec fn a1 a2 ... an := k, what do fn and a1 to an represent?","code":"","options":[],"answer":"fn is the name of the function and a1 to an are its parameters.","keywords":["name","function","parameters"],"explanation":"fn names the function, and the variables a1 to an are the parameters used in the body k."}]},{"steps":[{"say":"Let us go slowly now, step by step. Think of a function like a tea stall machine. You put in sugar and tea powder, and it gives you tea. The machine is made once and used many times.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Function = tea machine","body":""}]},{"say":"Step one. Write the word let. This tells the computer we are defining a function.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Step 1: write let","body":""}]},{"say":"Step two. Write the name of the function, for example square. Step three. Write the parameters, which are the inputs, for example x.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Step 2: name. Step 3: parameters","body":""}]},{"say":"Step four. Write colon equals, and then the formula. For square, the formula is x multiplied by x. The function returns that value.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Square","body":"let square x:=\n return: x * x"}]},{"say":"Step five. If the function calls itself, write let rec. For example, the factorial function calls itself, so it uses let rec.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Step 5: self-call means let rec","body":""}]},{"say":"Common mistakes. First, forgetting rec in a recursive function. Second, mixing up parameters and arguments. Third, forgetting the brackets when you write data type annotations. Brackets are compulsory there.","board":[{"kind":"warning","title":"Brackets needed for type annotations: (a: int)","body":""}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Function Definition and Syntax","question":"Which word starts a function definition in the textbook syntax?","code":"","options":["let","start","func","begin"],"answer":"let","keywords":[],"explanation":"A definition is introduced with the special word let."},{"id":"p2","type":"mcq","concept":"Function Definition and Syntax","question":"In let square x := x * x, what is x?","code":"","options":["A parameter","An argument value","The function name","A keyword"],"answer":"A parameter","keywords":[],"explanation":"x is a variable in the function definition, so it is a parameter."}]},{"steps":[{"say":"Let us start from the very basics. You already know a variable stores a value. For example a is assigned 24. This binds the value 24 to the name a. That is a definition.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Definition binds value to name","body":"a:=(24)"}]},{"say":"A function is also a definition. But instead of binding a fixed value, it binds a formula to a name. Like a rule written once and used many times.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Function = named rule","body":""}]},{"say":"A very simple example. The name is double. The input is x. The answer is x plus x.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Simple function","body":"let double x := x + x"}]},{"say":"Read it as: let, then name, then input, then colon equals, then the formula. If it calls itself, we add rec after let.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"let name inputs := formula","body":""}]},{"say":"Remember for exams. Parameters are in the definition. Arguments are the values we pass. Well done, you can do this!","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Argument","body":"Value passed to the function"}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"short","concept":"Function Definition and Syntax","question":"What is a recursive function?","code":"","options":[],"answer":"A function that calls itself is called a recursive function.","keywords":["calls itself","recursive"],"explanation":"The textbook defines a recursive function as one whose definition calls itself."},{"id":"p2","type":"mcq","concept":"Function Definition and Syntax","question":"What are the values passed to a function definition called?","code":"","options":["Arguments","Parameters","Subroutines","Modules"],"answer":"Arguments","keywords":[],"explanation":"Values passed are arguments; variables in the definition are parameters."}]}],"Interface vs Implementation":[{"steps":[{"say":"Let us learn this again with a new picture. Think of a restaurant. You are the customer. You read the menu and order a dosa. You do not enter the kitchen.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Interface vs Implementation","body":""}]},{"say":"The menu is the interface. It tells you what you can ask for. It does not cook anything by itself.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Interface","body":"The set of actions an object can do. It defines the actions but does not actually perform them."}]},{"say":"The kitchen is the implementation. The cook follows the steps and really makes the dosa. This is where the work happens.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Implementation","body":"Carries out the actions defined in the interface."}]},{"say":"Now a textbook example. A tube light or bulb has a switch. Anything that behaves like a bulb must have turn on and turn off functions. That list of functions is the interface. How the bulb actually glows is the implementation.","board":[{"kind":"example","title":"Bulb: turn_on() and turn_off() are the interface","body":""}]},{"say":"Another worked example is the car. The driver presses the accelerator and asks for speed 70. The accelerator is the interface between the driver and the engine. The engine burns fuel and makes power. That is the implementation, and it is hidden from the driver.","board":[{"kind":"diagram","title":"Car example","body":"flowchart TD\nA[\"Driver\"] --> B[\"Accelerator: speed(70)\"]\nB --> C[\"Engine does the work\"]"}]},{"say":"For the board exam, remember this. The interface defines what can be done. The implementation does it. They are kept separate. This is a favourite two mark question, so you can do it!","board":[{"kind":"warning","title":"Exam: Interface only defines actions; Implementation performs them","body":""}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Interface vs Implementation","question":"In the restaurant analogy, which one is the interface?","code":"","options":["The menu card","The kitchen","The cook","The cooking steps"],"answer":"The menu card","keywords":[],"explanation":"The menu tells what you can order, like an interface. The kitchen and cook do the real work, like the implementation."},{"id":"p2","type":"short","concept":"Interface vs Implementation","question":"In the car example, what is the interface and what is the implementation?","code":"","options":[],"answer":"The accelerator is the interface between the driver and the engine. The engine doing the actual work is the implementation.","keywords":["accelerator","interface","engine","implementation"],"explanation":"The driver uses only the accelerator. The engine's internal working is hidden and is the implementation."}]},{"steps":[{"say":"Let us go slowly now. Two words matter here. Interface and implementation. Let us understand each in simple words.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Two words: Interface, Implementation","body":""}]},{"say":"Step one. An interface is a list of things an object can do. It only says what. It does not do the work.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Interface = list of what can be done","body":""}]},{"say":"Step two. Implementation is the real work. It follows the instructions given in the interface.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Implementation = doing the work","body":""}]},{"say":"Here is a school analogy. The school timetable says which subject is in which period. That is like the interface. The teacher actually teaching in the class is the implementation.","board":[{"kind":"example","title":"Timetable = interface; Teaching = implementation","body":""}]},{"say":"Now the code example from the book. The function min takes three values x, y and z, and gives the smallest. A person who calls min only needs to know its name and inputs. The if conditions inside are the implementation.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"min function","body":"let min x y z :=\n if x < y then\n  if x < z then x else z\n else\n  if y < z then y else z"}]},{"say":"Common mistakes. First, do not say the interface does the work. It does not. Second, do not mix them up. The interface is the what, and the implementation is the how.","board":[{"kind":"warning","title":"Interface does NOT do the work","body":""}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Interface vs Implementation","question":"Which statement is correct?","code":"","options":["Implementation carries out the actions defined in the interface","Interface carries out the actions and implementation only lists them","Both are exactly the same thing","Interface is the hidden inner working of an object"],"answer":"Implementation carries out the actions defined in the interface","keywords":[],"explanation":"The interface only defines the actions. The implementation really performs them."},{"id":"p2","type":"short","concept":"Interface vs Implementation","question":"Using the school timetable analogy, explain interface and implementation in one or two sentences.","code":"","options":[],"answer":"The timetable only lists what happens in each period, so it is the interface. The teacher actually teaching is the implementation.","keywords":["timetable","interface","teacher","implementation"],"explanation":"The timetable defines what should happen but does not teach. The teaching is the real work, which is the implementation."}]},{"steps":[{"say":"Let us start from the very basics. You already know how to use a mobile phone. You use it without knowing its circuits. Keep this in mind.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Start: using without knowing inside","body":""}]},{"say":"On a phone you tap the call button. The button is what you use. This is like an interface.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Call button = interface","body":""}]},{"say":"Inside, circuits and software connect the call. You do not see this. This is like the implementation.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Circuits and software = implementation","body":""}]},{"say":"The same idea comes from the textbook. A bulb has a switch. Pressing it makes the bulb glow. You need not know how it glows.","board":[{"kind":"example","title":"Switch = interface; how it glows = implementation","body":""}]},{"say":"So remember one line. Interface tells what can be done. Implementation does it. Well done, you can get this right!","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Interface vs Implementation","body":"Interface defines actions. Implementation performs them."}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Interface vs Implementation","question":"A bulb has turn_on() and turn_off() functions. What are these functions called together?","code":"","options":["The interface","The implementation","The compiler","The side effect"],"answer":"The interface","keywords":[],"explanation":"The list of actions an object can do is its interface. How the bulb actually glows is the implementation."},{"id":"p2","type":"short","concept":"Interface vs Implementation","question":"On a phone, which part is the interface and which is the implementation?","code":"","options":[],"answer":"The call button you tap is the interface. The hidden circuits and software that connect the call are the implementation.","keywords":["button","interface","circuits","implementation"],"explanation":"The user only uses the button. The inner working that does the job is the implementation."}]}],"Parameters and Arguments":[{"steps":[{"say":"Let us look at this idea again with a new picture. Think of a school tiffin box with an empty slot. The slot is a parameter. The idli you put in today is the argument.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Parameters and Arguments","body":""},{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Parameter","body":"A variable written in the function definition. It is an empty slot."}]},{"say":"The textbook says the same. Parameters are the variables in the function definition. Arguments are the values passed to the function definition. The slot is fixed when we write the function. The value is filled in when we use it.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Argument","body":"A value passed to the function. It fills the slot."}]},{"say":"Now a worked example. Look at the function square. Its definition has one variable, x. So x is the parameter. Suppose we call square with the value 5. Then 5 is the argument. While the function runs, x holds 5.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Definition and call","body":"let square x :=\n    return: x * x\n\nsquare 5"},{"kind":"example","title":"x is the parameter, 5 is the argument","body":""}]},{"say":"Take the textbook function pow, with two parameters a and b. If we call pow with 2 and 3, then a gets 2 and b gets 3. The first argument goes to the first parameter. The second goes to the second.","board":[{"kind":"diagram","title":"Passing arguments","body":"flowchart TD\nA[\"Call: pow 2 3\"] --> B[\"a gets 2\"]\nA --> C[\"b gets 3\"]\nB --> D[\"Function body runs\"]\nC --> D"}]},{"say":"Remember for the board exam. Parameters are in the definition. Arguments are in the call. The textbook also says that when we write type annotations like a colon int, the brackets are compulsory.","board":[{"kind":"warning","title":"Do not mix them up: definition = parameter, call = argument","body":""}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Parameters and Arguments","question":"In the call fact 5 of the function let rec fact n, what is 5?","code":"","options":["A parameter","An argument","A function name","A data type"],"answer":"An argument","keywords":[],"explanation":"5 is the value passed to the function, so it is an argument. The variable n is the parameter."},{"id":"p2","type":"short","concept":"Parameters and Arguments","question":"In let min x y z, name the parameters. What are they called when we call min 4 9 2?","code":"","options":[],"answer":"The parameters are x, y and z. The values 4, 9 and 2 are the arguments.","keywords":["x y z","parameters","4 9 2","arguments"],"explanation":"Variables in the definition are parameters. The values given in the call are arguments."}]},{"steps":[{"say":"Let us go slowly now. Two words are confusing: parameter and argument. We will fix them with one simple picture.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Parameter vs Argument, step by step","body":""}]},{"say":"Think of a water bottle and the water. The bottle is empty and waiting. That is the parameter. The water you pour is the argument.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Bottle = parameter, water = argument","body":""}]},{"say":"Step one. When we write a function, we give names to the inputs. These names are parameters. Step two. When we use the function, we give real values. These values are arguments.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Write the function: parameters","body":""},{"kind":"point","title":"Use the function: arguments","body":""}]},{"say":"See this example. In the function sum x y, the names x and y are parameters. If we call sum 3 4, then 3 and 4 are arguments. The value 3 goes into x, and 4 goes into y.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Example","body":"let sum x y :=\n    return x + y\n\nsum 3 4"}]},{"say":"Common mistakes. First, calling the value a parameter. Second, thinking arguments are written inside the definition. Third, mixing up the order. The first value goes to the first name.","board":[{"kind":"warning","title":"Mistakes: swapping the two words, wrong order","body":""}]},{"say":"A quick check. Variables in the definition are parameters. Values passed in are arguments. Well done for trying again. You can do this!","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Argument","body":"The value passed to the function definition."}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Parameters and Arguments","question":"Which of these is written in the function definition?","code":"","options":["Parameter","Argument","Output","Answer"],"answer":"Parameter","keywords":[],"explanation":"Parameters are the variables in the function definition. Arguments are given only when the function is called."},{"id":"p2","type":"predict_output","concept":"Parameters and Arguments","question":"What does this call return? Treat the code like the textbook pseudocode.","code":"let double x :=\n    return: x * 2\n\ndouble 6","options":[],"answer":"12","keywords":[],"explanation":"The argument 6 goes to the parameter x, and 6 times 2 is 12."}]},{"steps":[{"say":"Let us start from the very basics. You already know a variable. A variable is a name that holds a value, like a marks in a box labelled maths.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Starting from variables","body":""}]},{"say":"A function is a small piece of code that does one job. To do its job, it often needs some input values. So we give it variables to hold those inputs.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"A function needs inputs to do its job","body":""}]},{"say":"The variables in the function definition are called parameters. This is the first thing to remember.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Parameter","body":"Variable in the function definition"}]},{"say":"The actual values we give while using the function are called arguments. This is the second thing to remember.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Argument","body":"Value passed to the function"}]},{"say":"A very simple example. In the function square x, x is the parameter. When we write square 4, the value 4 is the argument.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Simple example","body":"let square x :=\n    return: x * x\n\nsquare 4"}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Parameters and Arguments","question":"What are the values passed to a function definition called?","code":"","options":["Arguments","Parameters","Subroutines","Interfaces"],"answer":"Arguments","keywords":[],"explanation":"The textbook says arguments are the values passed to the function definition."},{"id":"p2","type":"short","concept":"Parameters and Arguments","question":"In let gcd a b, which names are parameters?","code":"","options":[],"answer":"The variables a and b are the parameters, because they are in the function definition.","keywords":["a","b","parameters","definition"],"explanation":"Variables written in the function definition are parameters."}]}],"Pure Functions":[{"steps":[{"say":"Let us look at pure functions again with a fresh idea. Think of a calculator button that squares a number. Press it with 5, you always get 25. Today, tomorrow, in any classroom. That is a pure function.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Pure Functions: a fresh look","body":""},{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Pure function","body":"Same arguments always give the same result, with no side effects."}]},{"say":"The textbook gives the example of square. It takes x and returns x times x. The answer depends only on x. Nothing outside the function can change it, and the function changes nothing outside.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"square from the textbook","body":"let square x:=\n return: x * x"}]},{"say":"Now compare with a school bell that rings at a time decided by the peon. You cannot predict it from your own input. In the same way, random gives a different answer every call, so it is impure.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Impure example","body":"let a:= random()\n if a > 10 then\n  return: a\n else\n  return: 10"}]},{"say":"Worked example. Call square with 4 three times. You get 16 each time. Now call inc with 5 when y is zero. Inc changes y to 5 and returns 5. Call it again and y becomes 10, so it returns 10. Same input, different output. So inc is impure.","board":[{"kind":"example","title":"square 4 gives 16 always; inc 5 gives 5, then 10","body":""}]},{"say":"For the board exam, remember two tests. First, same arguments, same answer. Second, no change to outside variables. Pass both and the function is pure. Also, strlen is pure, and sin of zero is always zero.","board":[{"kind":"warning","title":"Two tests: same output AND no side effects","body":""}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Pure Functions","question":"Which function is pure?","code":"","options":["random()","date()","sin(0)","a function that changes an outside variable"],"answer":"sin(0)","keywords":[],"explanation":"sin(0) is always 0 for the same input, so it is pure."},{"id":"p2","type":"short","concept":"Pure Functions","question":"Why is square, which returns x times x, a pure function?","code":"","options":[],"answer":"For the same input it always gives the same output and it changes nothing outside the function.","keywords":["same input","same output","no side effects"],"explanation":"Its result depends only on x, with no side effects."}]},{"steps":[{"say":"Let us go slowly, in very simple words. A pure function is like a tea stall rate board. One tea costs ten rupees. Ask for two teas, you pay twenty. Every single time.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Pure Functions, step by step","body":""}]},{"say":"Step one. A function takes input. These inputs are called arguments. Step two. It gives output. If the same input always gives the same output, that is the first sign of a pure function.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Step 1: same input gives same output","body":""}]},{"say":"Step three. A pure function must not touch anything outside itself. It should not change any outside variable. Changing outside things is called a side effect.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Step 2: no side effects","body":""}]},{"say":"Now an impure example. Here y is outside the function. Each call to inc adds x to y. So the answer keeps changing. Inc changes the outside variable y. That is a side effect.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Impure function inc","body":"y: = 0\nlet inc (x: int): int: =\n y: = y + x\nreturn (y)"}]},{"say":"Common mistakes. One, thinking side effects are always bad. The book says they can be useful. Two, thinking a function is impure only when it has parameters. Even without parameters, it can be impure, like random. Three, forgetting that pure functions can be replaced by one call if the arguments are the same.","board":[{"kind":"warning","title":"Side effect is not always harmful","body":""},{"kind":"warning","title":"No parameters does not mean pure","body":""}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Pure Functions","question":"A function changes the value of a variable outside itself. What is this called?","code":"","options":["Side effect","Parameter","Interface","Recursion"],"answer":"Side effect","keywords":[],"explanation":"Changing something outside the function is a side effect, which makes it impure."},{"id":"p2","type":"short","concept":"Pure Functions","question":"Give one example of an impure function from the textbook and say why it is impure.","code":"","options":[],"answer":"random() is impure because it gives different outputs for the same call.","keywords":["random","different output","same call"],"explanation":"The textbook gives random and date as impure examples."}]},{"steps":[{"say":"Let us build from what you already know. You know a function takes values and gives a value back. Like a machine. Put in something, get out something.","board":[{"kind":"heading","title":"Pure Functions: the basics","body":""}]},{"say":"If the machine always gives the same answer for the same input, we call it pure. Think of adding two numbers. Two plus three is always five.","board":[{"kind":"keyterm","title":"Pure function","body":"Same input, same output, every time."}]},{"say":"A very simple example is the square function. Square of three is nine. Always nine.","board":[{"kind":"code","title":"Simple pure function","body":"let square x:=\n return: x * x"}]},{"say":"If the answer can change for the same input, or the function changes outside variables, it is impure. Random is impure. Remember this one line for the exam: pure means same input, same output, no side effects.","board":[{"kind":"point","title":"Pure = same input, same output, no side effects","body":""}]}],"practice":[{"id":"p1","type":"mcq","concept":"Pure Functions","question":"What does a pure function give when called again with the same arguments?","code":"","options":["The same result","A different result each time","An error","A random number"],"answer":"The same result","keywords":[],"explanation":"A pure function depends only on its arguments, so the result is the same."},{"id":"p2","type":"short","concept":"Pure Functions","question":"Define a pure function in one sentence.","code":"","options":[],"answer":"A pure function always returns the same result for the same arguments and has no side effects.","keywords":["same arguments","same result","no side effects"],"explanation":"This is the textbook definition of a pure function."}]}]}};
